 **When to use:** When distinct structural components in a scalar field correspond to different intensity ranges and need to be visualized together [G].
 **When not to use:** When the number of isovales is very large, leading to excessive memory and rendering overhead [G].
 
 ## Background
-Originating from academic projects on data visualization, this method extends the classic Marching Cubes algorithm [10327-lookup-table-and-ambiguity-handling] to handle multiple thresholds. It is commonly used in medical imaging and scientific visualization to display different tissue types or material phases simultaneously.
